/home/sat/mcrl-v025-witness-ws/CONTROL-CEILING-FRAMING-2026-09-10.md:53:Report a feasible incumbent `L` and a valid upper bound `U`, or report only “best known.” A large feasible improvement establishes existence under the tested assumptions. A small best-known improvement establishes little about remaining room. A ceiling requires coverage or bounds over the entire declared action space, including the inner solve. Include C1 as a candidate whenever it is feasible. A worse search result then diagnoses search failure, not negative headroom.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-witness-ws/CONTROL-CEILING-FRAMING-2026-09-10.md:71:**Both provisioning rules need exact identities.** If assignment, admissible controls, physical evaluator, information and service constraints are identical, freely optimizing power and mode should give the same C2 optimum regardless of which provisioning rule supplied the initial guess. The two rules then supply different C1 references, not different physics ceilings. If each rule commits a different assignment or supplies a different BASELINE guard, separate conditional optima are legitimate, but their difference also includes those conditioning changes. If C2 remains constrained to a rule-specific power formula, call it a restricted policy-family search. A large gap confined to the defective rule measures defect cost, not residual opportunity in the corrected system.
