/home/sat/mcrl-v025-strategic-astra-ws/SWEEP-ASTRA-2026-09-10.md:109:**B. “Failure of a high-capacity model proves an informational ceiling.” Wrong. D.** The training gate G2 and [bakeoff metric 3][bakeoff] assert this. Finite-sample failure can arise from estimation, optimization, regularization, distribution shift, scaling or the tested model family. A collision witness can prove non-identifiability for those states; a validated distributional lower bound can quantify its prevalence. A failed fit cannot. Success would establish useful predictability on that held-out support, not a universal architectural diagnosis. The gate turns an inconclusive result into an impossibility proof and can stop useful work for the wrong reason.
